    {"id": "H-1", "page": "hub", "section": "paths", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-learning-paths", "w": 1792, "h": 1024, "alt": "Two lanes of twelve module tiles: the Nephrology Nursing path in numerical order and the Hemodialysis Nursing path in its own order, both ending at Module 12.", "caption": "Both paths use the same twelve modules. The Hemodialysis Nursing path moves to the dialysis modules (6 to 9) right after Module 1, and both paths finish with the Module 12 capstone."},
    {"id": "H-2", "page": "educator", "section": "structure", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-teaching-cycle", "w": 1792, "h": 1024, "alt": "Four-stage teaching cycle (mental model, physiology, evidence, bedside) feeding a nursing case framed as Assess, Communicate, Teach, Escalate, then a self-check.", "caption": "Every module moves from a plain-language picture to physiology, evidence, and bedside practice, then applies it in a nursing case (Assess, Communicate, Teach, Escalate) and ends with a self-check quiz with answer rationales."},
    {"id": "01-A", "page": "01", "section": "nephron", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-01-nephron-map", "w": 1792, "h": 1024, "alt": "Enlarged nephron with five labeled segments showing what each reclaims or controls, aldosterone and antidiuretic hormone at the collecting duct, and a small kidney locator.", "caption": "Each nephron filters first, then reclaims most of what it filtered. The table on this page adds the medicines that act at each segment."},
    {"id": "01-B", "page": "01", "section": "tests", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-01-two-kidney-tests", "w": 1792, "h": 1024, "alt": "Side-by-side panels: estimated filtration rate measures how much the kidneys filter; urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio measures leaking, each with caution tags.", "caption": "Estimated glomerular filtration rate (eGFR) shows how much the kidneys filter; the urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (ACR) shows how much albumin leaks through. Each predicts risk on its own, so a kidney check needs both."},
    {"id": "01-C", "page": "01", "section": "bedside", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-01-focused-renal-assessment", "w": 1792, "h": 1024, "alt": "Eight-step renal assessment from breathing to access site, ending in three judgments: stable, needs review today, or urgent escalation now.", "caption": "Start with breathing, because pulmonary congestion cannot wait. Weight is the most useful single number for fluid, and the assessment is complete only when it ends in a judgment and an action."},
    {"id": "01-D", "page": "01", "section": "balance", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-01-four-balances", "w": 1536, "h": 1152, "alt": "Reference grid for water, sodium, potassium, and acid balance: what the kidney controls, what goes wrong in kidney failure, and the clue to notice.", "caption": "Sodium sets the volume of fluid; water sets the sodium concentration. A swollen patient can have a low serum sodium, and deep, rapid breathing with clear lungs is a reason to check bicarbonate."},
    {"id": "02-A", "page": "02", "section": "causes", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-02-kdigo-risk-grid", "w": 1536, "h": 1152, "alt": "Color-coded grid of filtration by albuminuria categories showing risk level and suggested minimum kidney checks per year in each cell.", "caption": "Each cell gives the risk level and the suggested minimum number of kidney-function and albuminuria checks per year (KDIGO 2024). Sicker or less stable patients are seen more often."},
    {"id": "02-B", "page": "02", "section": "slowing", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-02-slowing-progression-pillars", "w": 1792, "h": 1024, "alt": "Six pillars holding up kidney protection: blood pressure, renin–angiotensin inhibitor, SGLT2 inhibitor, statin, sodium, and avoiding kidney harm, each with one nursing check.", "caption": "Nurses rarely prescribe these treatments, but they check whether each one is in place, tolerated, and restarted after an illness. A creatinine rise of up to 30% after starting a renin–angiotensin inhibitor is expected and is reported, not a reason to stop on its own."},
    {"id": "02-C", "page": "02", "section": "bedside", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-02-staging-at-the-desk", "w": 1024, "h": 1536, "alt": "Six-step workflow: review the trend, check for an acute change, establish chronicity, record cause and categories, reconcile medicines, and arrange monitoring or referral.", "caption": "A possible acute change is assessed promptly first; chronicity is confirmed only in a clinically stable patient, over at least 3 months. The kidney failure risk thresholds are KDIGO 2024 practice points, and local referral criteria may differ."},
    {"id": "03-A", "page": "03", "section": "physiology", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-03-four-disturbances", "w": 1792, "h": 1024, "alt": "Fewer working nephrons lead to anemia, mineral and bone disorder, potassium and acid retention, and poor nutrition, each linked to its body consequence.", "caption": "None of these disturbances announces itself with a dramatic symptom, so they are found by scheduled testing and by asking the right questions at every visit."},
    {"id": "03-B", "page": "03", "section": "mbd-care", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-03-mineral-bone-loop", "w": 1792, "h": 1024, "alt": "Loop showing retained phosphate and low active vitamin D lowering calcium, raising parathyroid hormone, and drawing minerals from bone, with six treatment entry points.", "caption": "Phosphate binders act in the gut and only work when taken with food. Trials show these treatments improve laboratory values more consistently than fractures or survival."},
    {"id": "03-C", "page": "03", "section": "diet", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-03-filipino-food-guide", "w": 1792, "h": 1024, "alt": "Filipino foods higher and lower in potassium, processed foods high in absorbed phosphate, a four-step potassium-lowering method, and protein advice by stage.", "caption": "These are counseling examples, not a complete table; portions and preparation are adjusted to each patient's own potassium and phosphate results. Unintentional weight loss calls for a dietitian, not more restriction."},
    {"id": "03-D", "page": "03", "section": "anemia-care", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-03-anemia-iron-decision", "w": 1024, "h": 1536, "alt": "Flowchart: gather blood count, reticulocytes, ferritin, and TSAT; suspend iron during infection; withhold iron when ferritin exceeds 700 or TSAT reaches 40%.", "caption": "Iron is held when ferritin is above 700 ng/mL or transferrin saturation (TSAT) is 40% or higher, and suspended during active infection (KDIGO 2026). Thresholds for starting iron differ by setting and follow the prescriber and unit protocol."},
    {"id": "04-A", "page": "04", "section": "physiology", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-04-aki-three-causes", "w": 1792, "h": 1024, "alt": "Kidney and urinary tract schematic dividing acute kidney injury causes into reduced blood supply, kidney tissue damage, and blocked urine outflow, with examples.", "caption": "Acute kidney injury is sorted like a slow household tap: the supply, the filter, or the outflow pipe. Reduced perfusion and obstruction are often reversible within hours if someone notices, so check them first."},
    {"id": "04-B", "page": "04", "section": "staging", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-04-aki-staging-card", "w": 1536, "h": 1152, "alt": "Reference card of KDIGO 2012 acute kidney injury definition and stages 1 to 3 by serum creatinine and urine output, with a 60 kg worked example.", "caption": "Any one definition criterion is enough to diagnose acute kidney injury, and the patient is placed in the highest stage reached by either creatinine or urine output. Even stage 1 is reported."},
    {"id": "04-C", "page": "04", "section": "evidence", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-04-hyperkalemia-sequence", "w": 1024, "h": 1536, "alt": "Algorithm showing nursing steps for hyperkalemia: recognize level and ECG, continuous monitoring, immediate SBAR call, prepare ordered treatments, recheck potassium.", "caption": "Emergency hyperkalemia treatment is prescribed; the nurse's time-critical work is recognition, continuous cardiac monitoring, an immediate call, and having ordered treatments ready. Because shifting treatments wear off, a repeat potassium is part of the plan."},
    {"id": "04-D", "page": "04", "section": "acute-krt", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-04-crrt-circuit", "w": 1672, "h": 941, "alt": "Continuous hemofiltration circuit from a right internal jugular dialysis catheter through blood pump and filter, replacement fluid route choice, effluent bag, and nursing watch points.", "caption": "In continuous hemofiltration, blood leaves through the red limb, is pumped up through the filter, and returns through the blue limb; replacement fluid joins one prescribed bloodline point, never the filter&rsquo;s side port. The catheter is drawn as a simple schematic, and the exact setup follows your device and unit."},
    {"id": "05-A", "page": "05", "section": "compare", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-05-four-roads", "w": 1792, "h": 1024, "alt": "Four equal columns comparing in-center hemodialysis, peritoneal dialysis, kidney transplant, and conservative kidney management by where, how often, who does the work, and daily life.", "caption": "Each pathway has its own toll, and none is right for everyone; the nurse's job is to make all four visible in the same level of detail. People often move between pathways over a lifetime."},
    {"id": "05-B", "page": "05", "section": "timing", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-05-preparation-timeline", "w": 1792, "h": 1024, "alt": "Timeline from stage 3 to the start of kidney replacement showing education, vein preservation, transplant referral, fistula, PD catheter, hepatitis B vaccination, and social work.", "caption": "Starting dialysis is a clinical decision made on symptoms and complications, not on a single number, so preparation happens early. An unplanned start through a temporary catheter is associated with more infections, longer hospital stays, and worse early survival."},
    {"id": "05-C", "page": "05", "section": "sdm", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-05-shared-decision", "w": 1792, "h": 1024, "alt": "Three-step shared decision conversation: introduce the choice, describe the options, help the patient weigh them, with nurse phrases and common fears.", "caption": "Shared decision-making runs in three steps, and the decision does not have to be made in one visit. Ask the patient early and privately how much they want to know and who they want involved."},
    {"id": "06-A", "page": "06", "section": "physiology", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-06-transport-processes", "w": 1792, "h": 1024, "alt": "Three panels showing diffusion as a tea bag, ultrafiltration as a coffee press, and convection, each mapped to the dialyzer membrane with driving force and what moves.", "caption": "Diffusion removes urea and potassium down a concentration gradient; ultrafiltration removes water by pressure; convection carries larger solutes along with that water. In the dialyzer, fresh dialysate flows opposite to the blood, so the gradient never runs out."},
    {"id": "06-B", "page": "06", "section": "circuit", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-06-hd-circuit-fistula", "w": 1672, "h": 941, "alt": "Hemodialysis circuit from forearm fistula arterial needle through pressure sensor, blood pump, heparin line, dialyzer, venous chamber, air detector, to venous needle, with countercurrent cutaway.", "caption": "Everything before the blood pump is pulled, so arterial pressure reads below zero; everything after is pushed, so venous pressure reads above zero. Blood and dialysate run in opposite directions through the dialyzer, and the air detector is the last guard before blood returns."},
    {"id": "06-C", "page": "06", "section": "circuit", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-06-pressure-alarms", "w": 1024, "h": 1536, "alt": "Reference card of four hemodialysis pressure alarms (arterial too negative, venous high, venous low, transmembrane high), each with likely causes and first checks.", "caption": "Each alarm is read on its own: find which side of the pump the sensor is on, then look at the patient and the access before touching the machine. The venous pressure alarm cannot be relied on to detect venous needle dislodgement, so the access stays visible for the whole treatment."},
    {"id": "06-D", "page": "06", "section": "dialysate", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-06-dialysate-components", "w": 1536, "h": 1152, "alt": "Reference card of seven dialysate components, sodium to temperature, with what each does and the risks if set too low or too high, including the calcium range.", "caption": "Dialysate is a prescribed drug delivered by the liter: the level set in the bath decides where the patient's blood level ends up. A wrong concentrate on the machine is a medication error and is reported as one."},
    {"id": "07-A", "page": "07", "section": "physiology", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-07-access-types", "w": 1792, "h": 1024, "alt": "Three panels compare an arteriovenous fistula, a graft, and a tunneled catheter by readiness, infection risk, and typical failure.", "caption": "A fistula takes weeks to months to mature but carries the lowest infection risk. A graft is ready sooner, and a tunneled catheter works immediately but has the highest rate of bloodstream infection. The \"rule of 6s\" is a traditional memory aid, not a test the access must pass."},
    {"id": "07-B", "page": "07", "section": "exam", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-07-look-feel-listen", "w": 1792, "h": 1024, "alt": "Infographic of normal fistula findings by look, feel, and listen, with six warning patterns, each paired with the nurse's action.", "caption": "A healthy fistula is soft, with a continuous thrill and a low-pitched continuous bruit. Each warning pattern points to a likely problem and an action, almost always to report it. A newly absent thrill and bruit means do not cannulate and notify the nephrologist promptly."},
    {"id": "07-C", "page": "07", "section": "cannulation", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-07-cannulation-techniques", "w": 1792, "h": 1024, "alt": "Three panels compare rope-ladder, area puncture, and buttonhole cannulation, with a strip showing needle direction and spacing along a fistula.", "caption": "Rope-ladder rotation spreads wear evenly and is the default for fistulas and the only technique for grafts. Area puncture balloons the vein into aneurysms. Buttonhole carries a higher infection risk and is limited to selected situations. The venous needle always points with the flow, toward the heart, and the two tips are kept well apart."},
    {"id": "07-D", "page": "07", "section": "catheters", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-07-catheter-care-sequence", "w": 1792, "h": 1024, "alt": "Step sequence for tunneled catheter connection and exit-site care, with two stop conditions: suspected catheter displacement and a break in asepsis.", "caption": "Organisms reach the blood mainly through the inside of the catheter, so hub care matters most. Stop if the catheter may have moved or if asepsis is broken; then follow the procedure and report. Antiseptic, scrub time, and lock solution follow unit procedure and the order."},
    {"id": "08-A", "page": "08", "section": "components", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-08-water-treatment-train", "w": 1792, "h": 1024, "alt": "Water treatment train from supply through filters, softener, two carbon tanks, reverse osmosis, and distribution loop to the dialysis machine, with failures and monitors.", "caption": "Carbon is the only barrier against chlorine and chloramine, and it gives no warning when exhausted, so total chlorine is tested after the carbon every treatment day. A machine showing normal conductivity and temperature can still be running on unsafe water. Sampling points and limits follow the unit water plan."},
    {"id": "08-B", "page": "08", "section": "syndromes", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-08-contaminant-syndromes", "w": 1536, "h": 1152, "alt": "Reference card with the cluster rule at center, surrounded by nine water contaminants and the symptoms each causes in patients.", "caption": "The nurse never sees a contaminant, only a patient with a symptom. One patient with chills usually has a patient-level cause. Two or more patients on the same shift with the same new symptom share an exposure, so stop connecting patients and investigate the common source first."},
    {"id": "08-C", "page": "08", "section": "disinfect-reuse", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-08-reprocessing-checkpoints", "w": 1792, "h": 1024, "alt": "Six safety checkpoints for a reprocessed dialyzer, from label and two-person identity check to the germicide absence test before priming.", "caption": "Each check answers a specific hazard: the validated disinfection process is verified, germicide presence before rinsing proves disinfection happened, and germicide absence after rinsing meets the release criterion. Any failed or missing check means the dialyzer is not used and the event is reported."},
    {"id": "09-A", "page": "09", "section": "physiology", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-09-session-three-phases", "w": 1792, "h": 1024, "alt": "Three-phase timeline of a dialysis session with nursing checks before, during, and after, plus findings that require a call before connecting.", "caption": "The pre-treatment assessment ends in one decision: start as prescribed, or call the physician first. During treatment, a half-minute round at each station checks the face, the access, the lines, and the machine. A patient leaves only when the discharge checks are met. All numeric thresholds come from unit policy or standing orders."},
    {"id": "09-B", "page": "09", "section": "bedside", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-09-idh-algorithm", "w": 1024, "h": 1536, "alt": "Algorithm for hypotension during dialysis: recognize, stop fluid removal, position, assess, notify, then escalate if no prompt recovery; prevention strategies alongside.", "caption": "Stopping fluid removal and raising the legs address the usual mechanism, and every further step follows protocol or order. Hypotension that does not correct promptly, or that comes with chest pain, arrhythmia, or altered consciousness, is treated as an emergency. A recurring pattern is a prescription problem to report."},
    {"id": "09-C", "page": "09", "section": "bedside", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-09-chairside-emergencies", "w": 1536, "h": 1152, "alt": "Reference card of five dialysis emergencies (air entry, hemolysis, needle dislodgement, dialyzer reaction, disequilibrium), each with signs and first actions.", "caption": "In every emergency, call for help early, stay with the patient, and record the onset time. In hemolysis and severe dialyzer reactions the blood in the circuit is not returned, and blood is never returned through a line that contains air. Oxygen, positioning, and further steps follow the unit&rsquo;s emergency protocol."},
    {"id": "10-A", "page": "10", "section": "membrane", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-10-pd-membrane", "w": 1792, "h": 1024, "alt": "Diagram of the peritoneal capillary wall with two kinds of opening, the fading glucose pull, and the shapes of fast and slow transporter curves.", "caption": "Water and small solutes cross the peritoneal capillary wall through gaps between cells, and water alone crosses through tiny water-only channels. Glucose leaks back into the blood, so the pull on water fades. This happens sooner in fast transporters, which is why they do better with short dwells."},
    {"id": "10-B", "page": "10", "section": "exchange", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-10-pd-exchange", "w": 1792, "h": 1024, "alt": "Five-step twin-bag peritoneal dialysis exchange shown as labeled flow paths (connect, drain, flush, fill, disconnect), with the reason for each step and how to record net removal.", "caption": "The flush comes before the fill because it carries any organisms picked up at the connection away to the drain bag before fluid flows toward the abdomen. Net fluid removal equals the volume drained minus the volume filled."},
    {"id": "10-C", "page": "10", "section": "infection", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-10-peritonitis-algorithm", "w": 1024, "h": 1536, "alt": "Five-step card for suspected PD peritonitis: contact the team now, assess and collect specimens, the three diagnostic criteria, prompt treatment, and reassessment.", "caption": "Cloudy fluid or abdominal pain means contacting the PD team now. Peritonitis is diagnosed when at least two of three criteria are met; specimens are collected before antibiotics when feasible, and prescribed treatment starts without waiting for culture results."},
    {"id": "11-A", "page": "11", "section": "physiology", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-11-transplant-anatomy", "w": 1792, "h": 1024, "alt": "Lower-abdomen diagram of a transplanted kidney joined to a pelvic artery, pelvic vein and bladder with a stent, labeled with each site's warning sign.", "caption": "The new kidney is placed in the lower abdomen and joined to the pelvic vessels and the bladder. Each join has its own complication. Because the graft has no nerve supply, a blocked ureter is often painless, and a clot in the artery can stop urine abruptly."},
    {"id": "11-B", "page": "11", "section": "complications", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-11-transplant-infection-timeline", "w": 1792, "h": 1024, "alt": "Three-window timeline of infections after kidney transplant (first month, months 1 to 6, after 6 months) with the nursing focus for each window.", "caption": "Infection risk follows the level of immunosuppression. Opportunistic infections peak in months 1 to 6 and come back whenever immunosuppression is increased to treat rejection. Fever in a recipient is always reported the same day."},
    {"id": "11-C", "page": "11", "section": "drugs", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-11-immunosuppressant-card", "w": 1536, "h": 1152, "alt": "Reference card of six transplant immunosuppressants showing what each does, side effects to ask about, and interactions to guard against. Shows no doses or levels.", "caption": "A vigilance card, not a prescribing guide. Doses and blood levels are set by the transplant team, and no new medicine, supplement or herbal product is started until the team has checked it."},
    {"id": "12-A", "page": "12", "section": "dashboard", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-12-pdsa-cycle", "w": 1792, "h": 1024, "alt": "Four-part Plan–Do–Study–Act cycle on early-ending afternoon sessions, with aim, measure and decision. All figures are labeled illustrative.", "caption": "A worked example with invented figures. One small change was tested on one shift for two weeks, measured weekly, and then kept and extended, and a second cycle began on cramps."},
    {"id": "12-B", "page": "12", "section": "dashboard", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-12-quality-dashboard", "w": 1536, "h": 1152, "alt": "Grid of ten dialysis-unit quality domains, each with example measures and no targets, plus the infection rate formula per 100 patient-months.", "caption": "A unit dashboard tracks a small set of measures every month. Each target comes from the guideline the unit has adopted and is set by the medical director. Accurate nursing records are where these reports begin."},
    {"id": "12-C", "page": "12", "section": "readiness", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-12-typhoon-readiness", "w": 1792, "h": 1024, "alt": "Three-phase typhoon readiness sequence for a dialysis unit (before, during, after) with the nurse's tasks and patient teaching for missed dialysis.", "caption": "Survival after a disaster is mostly arranged before it. Patients are dialyzed early when the nephrologist orders it, every patient is accounted for by name afterward with clinical urgency guiding who is dialyzed first, and treatment restarts only after the water system has been recommissioned and tested."},
    {"id": "12-D", "page": "12", "section": "teamwork", "stem": "nephrology-nursing-12-delegation", "w": 1792, "h": 1024, "alt": "Comparison of tasks a nurse may delegate in a dialysis unit, subject to authorization, training, policy, and supervision, and the judgments that stay with the registered nurse.", "caption": "A nurse may hand a task, not a judgment, to a trained and authorized technician or assistant under unit policy and supervision. Assessment, the decision to start, and evaluation of the response stay with the nurse, and so does the signature."},
